WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:02.676
- No one is ever perfect,
and just remember

00:00:02.676 --> 00:00:05.936
that progress is better than perfection.

00:00:05.936 --> 00:00:07.666
- I'm so excited that
you are gonna be doing

00:00:07.666 --> 00:00:10.775
this Zero-waste challenge with me.

00:00:10.775 --> 00:00:14.075
Are you down to show me your
trash from a regular day?

00:00:14.075 --> 00:00:18.824
- I bought some seaweed
snacks, sparkling water,

00:00:18.824 --> 00:00:21.334
root beer, and I ate this yesterday.

00:00:21.334 --> 00:00:24.113
This is all of my waste.

00:00:24.113 --> 00:00:26.473
- So, since you hardly make any trash

00:00:26.473 --> 00:00:28.193
and your already really low waste

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:31.512
maybe this can more be a tutorial

00:00:31.512 --> 00:00:34.592
and just showing people
how you avoid waste.

00:00:34.592 --> 00:00:35.492
- We need can do that.

00:00:35.492 --> 00:00:37.532
- I'm excited for you to show us

00:00:37.532 --> 00:00:40.094
how we can reduce waste
and be more low waste.

00:00:40.951 --> 00:00:42.871
- Hi everybody, it's Leah Thomas

00:00:42.871 --> 00:00:44.351
and I'm gonna be showing you all

00:00:44.351 --> 00:00:46.540
how I reduce waste and telling you

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.810
all about the waste that I do get.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:53.809
So here is my kind of
like Mason jar collection

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:56.319
its absolutely made my life a lot easier

00:00:56.319 --> 00:00:58.189
and that's just so easy to always have

00:00:58.189 --> 00:01:00.098
like containers to put stuff in.

00:01:00.098 --> 00:01:05.098
It's really what helps me not
really have a ton of waste.

00:01:05.828 --> 00:01:08.567
So what are some other things

00:01:08.567 --> 00:01:10.727
in my house that I can show you?

00:01:10.727 --> 00:01:15.726
I like to package a lot of my
food in these sorts of bags.

00:01:15.996 --> 00:01:19.166
This is a way that I also
stay low waste at my house.

00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:22.505
They come in all different
shapes and sizes.

00:01:22.505 --> 00:01:25.315
If I don't want to use plastic

00:01:25.315 --> 00:01:27.915
when I'm out and about,
I have things like this

00:01:27.915 --> 00:01:32.914
they have wooden forks and
knives and put cutlery.

00:01:33.164 --> 00:01:35.803
I try to be like super
free giving on myself.

00:01:35.803 --> 00:01:38.863
Like for example, I really
wanted some bagels, right?

00:01:38.863 --> 00:01:43.862
So I have this bagel
and it comes in plastic

00:01:44.462 --> 00:01:47.412
and I don't know, I need to eat.

00:01:47.412 --> 00:01:49.072
So when I really want bagels,

00:01:49.072 --> 00:01:50.391
and these happened to be the ones

00:01:50.391 --> 00:01:52.541
that were sold at Trader Joe's.

00:01:52.541 --> 00:01:55.431
- So we actually met when we did a video

00:01:55.431 --> 00:01:57.260
where you were explaining the intersection

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.570
between environmentaLism
and black lives matter.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:02.810
And since then, I know
you have started this

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:05.109
awesome thing called
intersectional environmentalist.

00:02:05.109 --> 00:02:06.859
Could you tell me a little
bit more about that?

00:02:06.859 --> 00:02:08.699
- Intersectional environmentalist

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:11.289
is an online platform that started

00:02:11.289 --> 00:02:13.118
during the summer of 2020.

00:02:13.118 --> 00:02:15.388
That's all about
exploring the relationship

00:02:15.388 --> 00:02:18.258
between social justice
and environmentalism

00:02:18.258 --> 00:02:21.117
because there are so many
links and interconnections.

00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:23.617
They're really trying
to make environmental

00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:25.627
education accessible to everyone.

00:02:25.627 --> 00:02:30.086
This is like a daily vitamin that I use

00:02:30.086 --> 00:02:32.376
and it comes in packaging that is

00:02:32.376 --> 00:02:34.875
compostable and biodegradable.

00:02:34.875 --> 00:02:37.585
And I just pour these refills into

00:02:37.585 --> 00:02:40.285
my glass container, so that way

00:02:40.285 --> 00:02:44.364
I don't have to go to
the store and you know,

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:46.854
buy a bunch of plastic
and things like that,

00:02:46.854 --> 00:02:50.072
even things like, you know, cotton swabs,

00:02:50.072 --> 00:02:52.433
just making sure that
I'm getting some sort

00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:56.222
of like easily recyclable packaging

00:02:56.222 --> 00:02:58.962
because the majority of consumer waste

00:02:58.962 --> 00:03:00.522
actually comes from packaging

00:03:00.522 --> 00:03:02.011
and that's pretty messed up.

00:03:02.011 --> 00:03:05.101
I'm primarily plant-based
and I just really

00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:08.661
need certain vitamins,
but I like to prioritize

00:03:08.661 --> 00:03:10.480
like glass containers because

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.590
those are really easily recyclable

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:14.720
- In the past, I've defined zero waste

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.339
as trying to send as little
as possible to landfill.

00:03:18.339 --> 00:03:20.589
I'm wondering how you
would define zero waste.

00:03:20.589 --> 00:03:22.429
- So I've been gravitating towards the

00:03:22.429 --> 00:03:25.138
terminology, low waste versus zero waste.

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:26.868
In reality, it's just saying, okay,

00:03:26.868 --> 00:03:29.498
if you have plastic,
making sure you're able

00:03:29.498 --> 00:03:32.097
to recycle it, doing as much as you can

00:03:32.097 --> 00:03:35.127
to divert any waste away from a landfill.

00:03:35.127 --> 00:03:37.907
To my surprise, I almost
felt like I kind of

00:03:37.907 --> 00:03:40.016
passed a test when I was talking

00:03:40.016 --> 00:03:42.986
to Aurin because currently, I'm one of

00:03:42.986 --> 00:03:46.775
the more low waste people
that she's talked to.

00:03:46.775 --> 00:03:48.805
Even as an environmental blogger,

00:03:48.805 --> 00:03:51.915
I get sent lots of things, so the majority

00:03:51.915 --> 00:03:55.724
of my waste is honestly
from packaging or from food.

00:03:55.724 --> 00:03:58.704
Just so you get a little sneak peek.

00:03:58.704 --> 00:04:01.813
Here are some of my packages that I get.

00:04:01.813 --> 00:04:06.253
Thankfully, a lot of it does
come in recycled packaging.

00:04:06.253 --> 00:04:08.482
I also donate a lot of those things

00:04:08.482 --> 00:04:11.642
to people who need it, I also gift my mom

00:04:11.642 --> 00:04:14.041
with a lot of things or like my employees

00:04:14.041 --> 00:04:16.321
or my coworkers and stuff like that.

00:04:16.321 --> 00:04:19.391
- How do you think this zero
waste movement can grow?

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:21.710
- Zero waste, like we
were talking about before

00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:23.850
it's been almost positioned as something

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.570
that only wealthy folks can do

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.029
and you can't buy your
way into sustainability.

00:04:29.029 --> 00:04:31.549
I don't really see a lot of representation

00:04:31.549 --> 00:04:34.129
of people of color or
people like my grandma.

00:04:34.129 --> 00:04:35.748
There are a lot of people who are living

00:04:35.748 --> 00:04:37.838
low waste out of necessity.

00:04:37.838 --> 00:04:40.428
It's actually kind of
funny how consumerism has

00:04:40.428 --> 00:04:42.807
infiltrated the zero waste circle.

00:04:42.807 --> 00:04:44.457
And that's really not what it's about.

00:04:44.457 --> 00:04:45.917
So I think that's kind of been a shift

00:04:45.917 --> 00:04:48.067
that I'm seeing while
am, you know, kind of

00:04:48.067 --> 00:04:50.636
transitioning more so
towards you know, low waste

00:04:50.636 --> 00:04:53.456
- The truth is zero waste is low waste.

00:04:53.456 --> 00:04:55.936
You can never be zero
waste, it's just like

00:04:55.936 --> 00:04:58.395
striving to do that, and it's funny too

00:04:58.395 --> 00:05:00.475
because the most zero waste thing

00:05:00.475 --> 00:05:03.365
is to not buy anything but then now

00:05:03.365 --> 00:05:06.624
there's this whole like
industry of products

00:05:06.624 --> 00:05:08.954
that have been built around supporting

00:05:08.954 --> 00:05:10.494
the zero waste lifestyle, which is

00:05:10.494 --> 00:05:13.003
I think sort of inherently not zero waste.

00:05:13.003 --> 00:05:16.833
- I like to use laundry detergent

00:05:16.833 --> 00:05:20.922
that doesn't really require a ton of waste

00:05:20.922 --> 00:05:23.742
whether it's things that
aren't using plastic.

00:05:23.742 --> 00:05:26.091
So something like this, it's from Dropps

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:27.891
and it comes in like a cardboard box

00:05:27.891 --> 00:05:31.431
which is really easy to
recycle it's unscented.

00:05:31.431 --> 00:05:34.290
So it's not using any
like toxic waste and dyes.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:36.550
I feel like this is kind of fun

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:39.550
as an environmentalist sometimes, honestly

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.509
like I always feel like
I'm not doing enough.

00:05:42.509 --> 00:05:45.139
And sometimes I feel
ashamed about the trash

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:47.598
that I do have but I
guess it's really nice

00:05:47.598 --> 00:05:49.178
and important to remind yourself

00:05:49.178 --> 00:05:50.978
of the things that you are doing.

00:05:50.978 --> 00:05:55.727
So that's a little bit
about how I try to reduce

00:05:55.727 --> 00:06:00.727
my waste around the house
I recycle when I can.

00:06:00.977 --> 00:06:02.456
And I try to make sure the packaging

00:06:02.456 --> 00:06:04.616
that I'm buying always has some sort of

00:06:04.616 --> 00:06:06.676
strategy for its end of life.

00:06:06.676 --> 00:06:09.026
And also not shaming
myself if I'm not perfect

00:06:09.026 --> 00:06:10.785
cause we're in a Panorama right now.

00:06:10.785 --> 00:06:12.425
So it's totally okay.

00:06:12.425 --> 00:06:14.125
- You're already really far along

00:06:14.125 --> 00:06:17.714
on your low waste journey and
being an environmentalist.

00:06:17.714 --> 00:06:20.394
What would you say to people

00:06:20.394 --> 00:06:21.974
who are starting on their journey?

00:06:21.974 --> 00:06:24.663
- You know, you can't buy
your way into sustainability.

00:06:24.663 --> 00:06:26.593
I don't want to confuse anyone to think

00:06:26.593 --> 00:06:29.043
that you have to like
be this mega consumer

00:06:29.043 --> 00:06:31.362
in order to be zero
waste because honestly,

00:06:31.362 --> 00:06:33.712
a lot of people probably
have all the tools

00:06:33.712 --> 00:06:35.362
and solutions already at home.

00:06:35.362 --> 00:06:38.801
- For people who have never heard the term

00:06:38.801 --> 00:06:41.091
intersectional environmentalism.

00:06:41.091 --> 00:06:43.036
Can you explain what that is?

00:06:43.036 --> 00:06:45.270
- Intersectional environmentalism

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.930
in essence just means taking an approach

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:51.160
to environmentalism that
also evaluates, you know,

00:06:51.160 --> 00:06:55.269
class, race, gender, sexuality, etcetera,

00:06:55.269 --> 00:06:57.139
and also accessibility.

00:06:57.139 --> 00:06:59.349
- And how do we find out more

00:06:59.349 --> 00:07:01.748
about intersectional environmentalist?

00:07:01.748 --> 00:07:03.215
- You can check out our website

00:07:03.215 --> 00:07:05.728
@intersectionalenvironmentalist.com.

00:07:05.728 --> 00:07:08.808
Also our Instagram
@intersectionalenvironmentalist.

00:07:08.808 --> 00:07:11.391
(upbeat music)

